{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QqmLsqBHuoFuiE5BMuyLNe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t+++\n2\tid = \"WI-579\"\n3\ttitle = \"The verdict carrier and the adjudication_review dial: gate over logged rounds, tree-bound trailer, generated rollup\"\n4\tworkstream = \"process\"\n5\tspecref = \"\"\n6\tbuildtier = \"strong\"\n7\tpriority = 9\n8\tsafety_class = \"ordinary\"\n9\tsupersedes = \"WI-558;WI-559;WI-560\"\n10\t+++\n11\t\n12\t## Deliverable\n13\t\n14\tThe merge gate reads the EVIDENCE and the evidence names the tree it judged.\n15\t\n16\t`kitlib/verdict.py` (LLR-207, IF-175) is the verdict record's one home: the\n17\tnon-record tree identity — a SHA-256 fold of `git ls-tree -r -z` with\n18\t`docs/reviews/`, `docs/log.d/` and `docs/iteration/` dropped — the\n19\t`Review-Verdict: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED rounds=<N> tree=<64 hex>` trailer\n20\tgrammar, the round-file / session-log join, and the branch-scoped readers. Two\n21\tcallers stand on it and that is the point: `integrate._verdict_gate` (may this\n22\tmerge?) and `agent_loop.review_owed_by_evidence` (does this lane still owe a\n23\tround?) asked one question with two rules over two exclusion sets, and the gap\n24\tbetween them drew two identical rounds on WI-547.\n25\t\n26\t- **WI-558 DW1** — the gate computes over the branch's own round files,\n27\t  restricted to rounds a LOGGED reviewer session produced: a file under\n28\t  `docs/reviews/` is a round only when the coordinator's own committed session\n29\t  log for that (train, ordinal) declares a REVIEW phase. Finding K closed —\n30\t  an implementer cannot author its own approval without also forging the loop's\n31\t  telemetry.\n32\t- **WI-558 DW2** — the machine half rides the round's own record commit as a\n33\t  trailer, written by the COORDINATOR and verified against the identity its\n34\t  carrier GOVERNS (the `Bar-Green` pattern, through the same peel the readers\n35\t  use, so the writer stamps the value they key on). GOVERNING = TREE IDENTITY: a verdict counts only\n36\t  while it names the branch's current non-record tree, read at the GOVERNING\n37\t  REV so neither the station refresh nor the commit that records the round\n38\t  stales an honest APPROVE. The freshness\n39\t  comparison is gone; there is no ordering rule left. The trailer is a\n40\t  CROSS-CHECK and never an accept path — one that contradicts the rounds\n41\t  refuses the merge.\n42\t- **WI-558 DW3** — `gen_verdict_rollup.py` (LLR-208) rebirths the rollup as a\n43\t  GENERATED artifact: `docs/stack.ini [generated]`, `trunk_step.py --regen`, a\n44\t  `check.py verdict-rollup` freshness step on the hook floor and in\n45\t  `_TRUNK_FRESHNESS_STEPS`, and `--check` with two answers. The gate never reads\n46\t  it, and the file says so where a human will see it.\n47\t- **WI-558 DW4** — the migration window: the gate accepts EITHER the round\n48\t  evidence or a legacy hand-authored rollup, judged by the SAME identity rule,\n49\t  warning on stderr whenever the legacy path is what cleared it. Two\n50\t  `RESYNC_PACK.md` entries. The trailer is additive and costs an adopter nothing.\n51\t- **WI-558 DW5 / WI-560 DW4 / WI-559 DW3** — `tests/test_verdict_record.py`\n52\t  drives each half beside its opposite (TC-205, TC-206).\n53\t- **WI-560 DW1** — ONE definition of \"the last commit that could invalidate a\n54\t  verdict\", used by the merge slot AND the C2 review-owed derivation. That\n55\t  definition names a REV as well as a path set, so the station-refresh peel\n56\t  (`refresh_attestation` / `work_tip`) lives beside the fold and both readers\n57\t  call `governing_identity` rather than each choosing where to measure. The\n58\t  double-identical-round class is now unrepresentable, not policed: the commits\n59\t  that caused it cannot move the identity either reader compares.\n60\t- **WI-559 DW2** — a committing ADJUDICATE session schedules its round exactly\n61\t  as a committing BUILD does, under the dial; the phase stays in\n62\t  `NON_BUILD_PHASES` because a judgement is not a build. No exit banner claims a\n63\t  round that was never drawn — it counts them.\n64\t- **The dial** — `docs/process.toml [attestation] adjudication_review`\n65\t  (`never` / `when-minting` / `always`; template and this repo ship\n66\t  `when-minting`), read through `agent_common.adjudication_review_owed` by BOTH\n67\t  the scheduler and the gate, with a closed vocabulary refused at preflight and\n68\t  a conservative fallback. Its `docs/enforcement-audit.md` row and the\n69\t  `RESYNC_PACK.md` entry ship with it. An adjudication lane drafting ordinary\n70\t  successors now merges with NO verdict artifact present anywhere — the OI-76\n71\t  acceptance becomes measurable on the next unattended run.\n72\t\n73\tReview A rework closed both evidence-boundary gaps. The loop's resume reader now\n74\tuses the same branch-scoped committed-path, logged-session and tree-bound entry\n75\tpipeline as the merge gate, so an approval-shaped file produced by a BUILD\n76\tsession cannot suppress the round the gate requires. The trailer's `rounds=N`\n77\tfield is now defined and checked as the completed review cycles represented at\n78\tthe governing tree (a dual-review cycle counts once, and rework starts the\n79\ttree-scoped count over); both the coordinator writer and gate derive that value\n80\tfrom the shared evidence. LLR-207's rationale now carries only the standing\n81\ttechnical reason, without the decision's provenance narrative.\n82\t\n83\tReview A round 007 closed four more, three of them one shape — a rule expressed\n84\tas a value something else got to choose. The identity's REV is now part of the\n85\tshared definition (the peel moved into `kitlib/verdict.py`, so a station refresh\n86\tno longer makes the two readers disagree); `branch_trailers` returns the ordered\n87\tSEQUENCE of attestations per tree, so the newest-first `git log` can no longer\n88\thand a reader a superseded stamp and have the cross-check accuse an approved\n89\tlane of forgery; and `tree_identity` passes `-z` while `fold_listing` takes\n90\tdecoded entries, so a quoted non-ASCII path can no longer fold a record file\n91\tinto the identity. The DONE banner states rounds DRAWN this run and the latest\n92\tverdict, because the tally it reads holds every completed round whatever its\n93\toutcome.\n94\t\n95\tVerifying finding 2's fix end-to-end found a fifth defect of the same family,\n96\tdriven and closed here. The refresh peel was TIP-ONLY, because `work_tip` is\n97\tshared with the `reset --hard` in `integrate.refresh` where peeling one commit\n98\ttoo far destroys work. So a telemetry commit — a `docs/iteration/` record path,\n99\twhich the fold exists to ignore — moved the governing identity anyway, by\n100\tBURYING the refresh under it: the loop answered `owed=True` and the merge slot\n101\trefused \"no logged review round names its current tree\", both readers agreeing\n102\ton the wrong answer and parking an honest APPROVE at a supervisor stop. That is\n103\tthe OI-76 failure mode itself, one commit further down than round 007 reached.\n104\t`governing_rev` now walks past that commit to reach the refresh, and `work_tip`\n105\tkeeps its tip-only contract untouched — the two shapes of the peel are separate\n106\tbecause a read-only reader can afford an answer a destructive one cannot.\n107\t\n108\tRound 012 found the same defect one commit SHAPE further on, and closing it\n109\tdeleted a rule rather than adding a case. The walk stepped by classifying the\n110\tPATHS a commit touched, so every commit whose paths it could not read — a merge,\n111\tan empty commit — stopped it. `commit_telemetry` writes exactly that shape: a\n112\t`Review-Verdict:` attestation must land even when the bookkeeping it rides is\n113\tunchanged, so it commits EMPTY, and the very commit RECORDING an approval buried\n114\tthe refresh underneath it. The step condition is now the module's own defining\n115\tsentence — a commit whose non-record identity EQUALS its first parent's cannot\n116\tinvalidate a verdict — measured directly from `tree_identity`. The empty\n117\tcarrier, the merge commit and the quoted-path trap stop being cases, because no\n118\tpath is classified in the walk at all. The regression drives the carrier through\n119\t`commit_telemetry` itself rather than a hand-made lookalike, and\n120\tPROCESS_OPTIONS.md's normative sentence is qualified to match the rule stated\n121\tbeside it: a commit that changes the non-record tree buys another round.\n122\t\n123\tRound 015 found the last place that computed the definition instead of asking\n124\tfor it — the BINDINGS. Round 007 moved the rev into the shared definition and\n125\tround 012 made the walk's step condition that definition's own sentence, but\n126\t`round_entries` still bound a round by `tree_identity(reviewed sha)`, and\n127\t`branch_trailers` and the coordinator's `review_verdict_trailer` each chose\n128\ttheir own rev too. Those agree everywhere except across a refresh, which is the\n129\tone class the peel exists for: a round drawn AFTER a station refresh cites the\n130\tPOST-refresh sha and is governed by the PEELED tree, so the two were permanently\n131\tunequal, the round was invisible to BOTH readers, and the lane re-drew an\n132\tidentical one every tick — the class WI-560 Done-when 1 claims to make\n133\tunrepresentable, re-entered through the binding rather than through the rev, and\n134\treachable on the shipped path because a lane's refresh is spawned before the\n135\tmerge slot runs. `governing_rev`/`governing_identity` now take the rev as an\n136\targument and every reader asks through them; the second rev-choice is deleted,\n137\tnot compensated for. Two artifacts were reporting states they could not produce\n138\tor clear: `commit_telemetry`'s empty-carrier arm dropped the pathspec and so\n139\tcommitted THE INDEX under a `telemetry:` label — the attestation's \"changes no\n140\ttree\" premise simply false — and `gen_verdict_rollup --check` called an EXTRA\n141\trollup stale while the write path never removed one, an unbreakable red on the\n142\thook floor under an instruction that could not work. The generator now OWNS\n143\t`docs/reviews/rollup/`, and the carrier's path scope is STATED by `--only`\n144\trather than implied by whether a pathspec is present. That last word is a\n145\tround-018 correction to a round-015 fix: keeping the pathspec on both arms of\n146\tthe `dirty` test still appended it only `if rels`, so `paths=[]` — the call\n147\tthis repo's own suite makes twice — went on emitting a pathspec-less\n148\t`git commit --allow-empty` and sweeping the index into the attestation's\n149\tcarrier, driven. A scope conditional on the shape of its input is the same\n150\tdefect as no scope; `--only` holds on every arm, and git-commit(1) documents it\n151\twith `--allow-empty` as creating the empty commit that arm needs. TC-205 and\n152\tTC-206 had each ENUMERATED an arm no test drove — which is precisely why both\
